  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1240 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery is dominated by the deep closed low spinning\nover the Appalachians. That system is making headlines by \ndisrupting holiday plans on the East Coast. Here in the Great \nPlains, a sfc trof is slicing through the Dakotas and into \neastern Nebraska. Partly cloudy skies are giving way to overcast\nconditions. Under today's increasing cloud cover, temps should \npeak near 50F... just above seasonal norms and noticeably warmer\nthan the past 5 days. Radar returns suggest widespread \nflurries/sprinkles, but perusal of regional obs shows that \nvery little is hitting the ground. Forecast soundings show 10kft\nof low-level dry air to overcome. The light returns aren't \nexpected to saturate that air... meaning most of those radar \nreturns will be evaporating before reaching the populace. Have \nbrought 20% PoPs down to 10% as a result. That minor threat \nshould end by sunset.\n\nOvernight lows will also be warmer than the past with the exception \nof northeastern Nebraska where some light snowcover remains.",
